  "body": "## What happens\n\n- The probe alarms write `#3000=1`, `#3000=2` and `#3000=3`. The Mazak EIA/ISO programming manual says that alarm numbers 1-20 show a fixed system message. Thus the control does not show our text (\"PROBE NO CONTACT\" and the others). The program still stops.\n- The option \"First sequence number\" accepts up to 999999, and `nextSeq()` has no limit. The manual permits five digits after N. With \"Every block\" and a step of 10, a program of more than 9,999 blocks writes N numbers past N99999.\n\n## Expected\n\n- The probe alarms use numbers of 21 or more, so the control shows our message.\n- The N number stays at or below 99999. It wraps, or the option maximum is 99999 and the post refuses a longer program by name.\n\n## Evidence (upstream main `eafe6aec6`)\n\n- `assets/posts/mazak.cps:1032`, `:1065`, `:1084`: the alarm numbers 2, 3 and 1.\n- `assets/posts/mazak.cps:41`: `sequenceNumberStart` max 999999.\n- `assets/posts/mazak.cps:168-172`: `nextSeq()`.\n- Related: #4148 (the same N limit for Mach3 and Mach4).\n\nFound by the post audit against the Mazak manuals (#4130).\n\n## Done means\n\n- The probing goldens show alarm numbers of 21 or more.\n- A test pins N at or below 99999 for a long program.\n",
